#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""load_balancer.py -- Person 4: TCP load balancer (static + adaptive).

A transparent TCP proxy. Clients connect to the load balancer (default
10.0.0.4:5000); for every new connection it picks a backend server
(10.0.0.11/12/13:6000), tells the SDN controller which backend was chosen,
connects to it and relays bytes both ways. Because it only relays bytes it does
not depend on the request/response format used by Person 3's clients/servers.

Modes (same code, same logs, only the selection rule differs):
  static   : round-robin over servers that are not down.
  adaptive : lowest load score among servers that are up and not overloaded.

Adaptive score (0..1, lower is better), see SCORE section below:
  0.35 * connections   active + reserved (picked but not yet connected)
  0.25 * latency       moving average of time-to-first-byte seen by the LB
  0.20 * traffic       recent bytes/s relayed to that backend
  0.20 * health        health-check round-trip time

Health checks: every second a probe connects to each backend, sends STATUS and
reads the reply. 3 failed probes -> down. A refused/timed-out connect while
proxying marks the server down immediately and the request is retried on
another server. 2 good probes -> up again.

Overload: a server is overloaded when (active + reserved) >= --overload-conns
or its score >= 0.80. It stays excluded until it falls below 70% of the
connection limit AND its score is <= 0.60 (hysteresis, avoids flapping). If all
servers are overloaded the least-loaded one is still used.

Controller notification: one JSON line over UDP per selection, e.g.
  {"type":"backend_selected","ts":1730550000.1,"mode":"adaptive",
   "lb_ip":"10.0.0.4","client_ip":"10.0.0.1","client_port":41234,
   "backend":"server2","backend_ip":"10.0.0.12","backend_port":6000}

Logs (in --log-dir, files prefixed lb_<mode>_<run-id>_):
  decisions.csv     one row per selection (scores of every server at that time)
  requests.csv      one row per proxied connection (connect/ttfb/duration/bytes)
  events.csv        down / up / overload_on / overload_off / start / stop
  network_load.csv  once per second: interface rx/tx rate, utilisation, per-
                    backend traffic rate and active connections
"""

import argparse
import csv
import json
import os
import selectors
import signal
import socket
import sys
import threading
import time

